1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,878
Research Computing on Explorer and AICR. Optimizing GPU Usage on AICR. How to

2
00:00:04,898 --> 00:00:10,153
request the right GPU, measure what your job actually uses, and right-size it, so

3
00:00:10,173 --> 00:00:12,790
your work runs sooner and wastes nothing.

4
00:00:12,860 --> 00:00:17,359
Bonjour. This is Liam, in for Bear. First, a correction. A GPU job that finished is

5
00:00:17,379 --> 00:00:22,443
not a GPU job that used the GPU. Many jobs leave the card waiting most of the time.

6
00:00:22,463 --> 00:00:26,962
This lecture is a set of measuring instruments: how to confirm you have the GPU, how

7
00:00:26,982 --> 00:00:31,764
to watch it live, how to profile it, how to fix the usual bottleneck, how to read

8
00:00:31,784 --> 00:00:35,718
memory against utilization, when to add GPUs, and the loop that ties it all

9
00:00:35,738 --> 00:00:37,130
together. Measure first. Change second.

10
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:42,006
Six terms. GPU utilization: the share of the last second in which the GPU was

11
00:00:42,026 --> 00:00:47,154
running any work at all. VRAM: the GPU's own memory, where your model and your batch

12
00:00:47,174 --> 00:00:51,980
live. DataLoader: the part of PyTorch that reads and batches your data and hands it

13
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,807
to the GPU. Profiler: a tool that says where the time went. BF16: a sixteen-bit

14
00:00:56,827 --> 00:01:00,668
number format that keeps the range of thirty-two-bit floats. And DDP: distributed

15
00:01:00,688 --> 00:01:02,920
data parallel, one model copy per GPU.

16
00:01:02,990 --> 00:01:07,331
Start where the work can quietly go wrong. If the CUDA module isn't loaded, PyTorch

17
00:01:07,351 --> 00:01:12,854
runs on the CPU and raises no error at all. A typo in the GPU request lands you on

18
00:01:12,874 --> 00:01:17,214
the wrong kind of node. And a leftover CUDA visible devices setting from an earlier

19
00:01:17,234 --> 00:01:19,540
shell can hide the card you were given.

20
00:01:19,610 --> 00:01:25,384
AICR has two GPU families and four GPU partitions. B200 devel and B200 batch, with

21
00:01:25,404 --> 00:01:30,792
one hundred ninety-two gigabytes of memory per card. RTX devel and RTX batch, with

22
00:01:30,812 --> 00:01:35,427
ninety-six. Devel partitions run four hours and are for interactive work and

23
00:01:35,447 --> 00:01:39,290
profiling. Batch partitions run twenty-four hours and are for production.

24
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:44,801
A minimal job script. The request first: one node, one task, eight CPUs per task,

25
00:01:44,821 --> 00:01:49,170
one GPU, thirty-two gigabytes of memory, four hours, and the log files.

26
00:01:49,240 --> 00:01:54,158
Then the work. Make the logs folder, clear the modules, load CUDA and miniforge,

27
00:01:54,178 --> 00:01:56,980
activate the workshop environment, and run your training.

28
00:01:57,050 --> 00:02:01,895
Two lines deserve a pause. Mem equals thirty-two gigabytes is CPU memory, not GPU

29
00:02:01,915 --> 00:02:07,455
memory. The GPU's own memory comes with the GPU. And CPUs per task is not a

30
00:02:07,475 --> 00:02:11,972
formality: your data loading workers run on those cores. Eight cores means seven

31
00:02:11,992 --> 00:02:13,710
workers and one main process.

32
00:02:13,780 --> 00:02:18,040
Never trust that you got what you asked for. The workshop's verify script runs in

33
00:02:18,060 --> 00:02:22,035
under five seconds and checks five things. Is CUDA reachable. How many GPUs, which

34
00:02:22,055 --> 00:02:26,601
model, how much memory. What CUDA visible devices says. Does NVIDIA S M I agree. And

35
00:02:26,621 --> 00:02:31,452
a tensor round trip: CPU to GPU and back. Is available only means PyTorch can see a

36
00:02:31,472 --> 00:02:34,020
GPU. The round trip proves it can use it.

37
00:02:34,090 --> 00:02:38,746
About that variable. Slurm sets CUDA visible devices to tell your program which GPUs

38
00:02:38,766 --> 00:02:43,756
are yours. Zero means one GPU. Zero comma one means two. Not set means you're

39
00:02:43,776 --> 00:02:48,432
outside Slurm, in an interactive shell, which is normal. Never override it in your

40
00:02:48,452 --> 00:02:50,770
script unless you deliberately want a subset.

41
00:02:50,840 --> 00:02:55,297
One AICR-specific rule. A background process watches GPU utilization, and a job that

42
00:02:55,317 --> 00:03:00,462
holds a GPU without running anything is cancelled. So don't request a GPU and then

43
00:03:00,482 --> 00:03:05,283
sit writing code. Run the verify script first, profile in the devel partition in

44
00:03:05,303 --> 00:03:09,760
short sessions, and keep the batch partitions for jobs that are already tuned.

45
00:03:09,830 --> 00:03:14,803
The fastest profiler is already on every node. NVIDIA S M I prints a snapshot; watch

46
00:03:14,823 --> 00:03:18,548
it every second in a second terminal. Two numbers matter. GPU utilization:

47
00:03:18,568 --> 00:03:22,292
eighty-four percent here means the GPU was running kernels for eighty-four percent

48
00:03:22,312 --> 00:03:26,037
of the last second. Healthy. Below fifty, something is wrong. And memory:

49
00:03:26,057 --> 00:03:29,470
twenty-four gigabytes of one hundred ninety-two in use. Twelve percent. Enormous

50
00:03:29,490 --> 00:03:31,030
room for a bigger batch.

51
00:03:31,100 --> 00:03:34,461
A quick quiz from the workshop. Utilization twelve percent, memory ninety-one

52
00:03:34,481 --> 00:03:38,765
percent. What's wrong? The model is loaded, the card is full, and it's barely

53
00:03:38,785 --> 00:03:43,376
computing. Nothing on the CPU side is feeding it. That is data starvation, the most

54
00:03:43,396 --> 00:03:46,450
common problem of all, and it gets its own act.

55
00:03:46,520 --> 00:03:50,224
Hold these two numbers as two axes, because people confuse them. Across:

56
00:03:50,244 --> 00:03:54,568
utilization. Up: memory. Top left is a loaded model that isn't running: a data

57
00:03:54,588 --> 00:03:59,222
pipeline problem. Bottom left is a GPU doing nothing at all. Bottom right is good

58
00:03:59,242 --> 00:04:03,877
throughput with memory to spare. Top right is the target: a big model, working hard.

59
00:04:03,897 --> 00:04:07,290
Eighty to a hundred percent utilization, seventy to ninety percent memory.

60
00:04:07,360 --> 00:04:12,559
For a richer live view, N V I top. A dashboard that updates continuously, per

61
00:04:12,579 --> 00:04:17,431
process: GPU use, memory trend, power and temperature. On a shared node it answers

62
00:04:17,451 --> 00:04:22,302
the question that matters: is it my Python process using the GPU, or someone's

63
00:04:22,322 --> 00:04:22,650
leftover.

64
00:04:22,720 --> 00:04:28,157
NVIDIA S M I tells you that there's a problem. A profiler tells you where the time

65
00:04:28,177 --> 00:04:34,256
goes. There are layers. System level: NVIDIA S M I and N V I top. Is the GPU busy.

66
00:04:34,276 --> 00:04:38,428
Framework level: the PyTorch profiler. Which operator or layer eats the GPU time.

67
00:04:38,448 --> 00:04:42,601
Timeline: N-sight Systems. Every CPU thread, kernel and memory copy in order. And

68
00:04:42,621 --> 00:04:46,132
kernel level: N-sight Compute. Is one kernel memory-bound or compute-bound. Most

69
00:04:46,152 --> 00:04:48,700
problems show up on the first two rungs.

70
00:04:48,770 --> 00:04:53,880
The habit that matters most on AICR: profile in devel, run production in batch. In a

71
00:04:53,900 --> 00:04:59,010
B200 devel session you run the verify script, run your code for fifty to a hundred

72
00:04:59,030 --> 00:05:02,858
steps, profile, fix the bottleneck, and profile again until utilization is above

73
00:05:02,878 --> 00:05:07,667
seventy percent. Only then does the job go to B200 batch, unattended, and you read

74
00:05:07,687 --> 00:05:08,950
jobstats when it finishes.

75
00:05:09,020 --> 00:05:14,209
The PyTorch profiler needs no extra install. Open a profile that records CPU and

76
00:05:14,229 --> 00:05:19,418
CUDA activity, records the tensor shapes, and writes a trace you can open in

77
00:05:19,438 --> 00:05:19,790
TensorBoard.

78
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:24,148
Then the pattern that matters: profile a short window, never the full run. Twenty

79
00:05:24,168 --> 00:05:28,455
steps is enough. Step the profiler each iteration, and print the table sorted by

80
00:05:28,475 --> 00:05:31,840
total CUDA time. The top row is where you look first.

81
00:05:31,910 --> 00:05:37,152
Reading it, in order. Gaps between kernels on the timeline: the GPU is idle, the CPU

82
00:05:37,172 --> 00:05:42,086
is too slow. Large copy entries: data shuttling between CPU and GPU. CPU total far

83
00:05:42,106 --> 00:05:47,019
above CUDA total: the work isn't really on the GPU. And DataLoader functions at the

84
00:05:47,039 --> 00:05:49,650
top of the CPU table: the pipeline, again.

85
00:05:49,720 --> 00:05:53,513
When the order of events matters, CPU threads, kernels, memory copies, communication

86
00:05:53,533 --> 00:05:59,232
between GPUs, use Nsight Systems. Trace CUDA, NVTX, the OS runtime, c u D N N and c

87
00:05:59,252 --> 00:06:04,634
u BLAS, write the report to scratch, and copy it to your laptop for the N-sight GUI.

88
00:06:04,654 --> 00:06:09,400
Limit the capture to fifty or a hundred steps: a full run makes gigabyte files.

89
00:06:09,470 --> 00:06:13,363
Now the number one bottleneck. Picture a high-speed factory: the B200. It finishes a

90
00:06:13,383 --> 00:06:16,996
batch in eight milliseconds. The supply truck, the CPU data loader, takes fifty

91
00:06:17,016 --> 00:06:20,909
milliseconds to bring the next one. The factory stops and waits. Nothing is wrong

92
00:06:20,929 --> 00:06:25,380
with the factory. The supply chain can't keep up. And a B200 eats batches far faster

93
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:30,132
than the cards you may be used to, so a loader that barely kept up elsewhere will

94
00:06:30,152 --> 00:06:30,970
starve it here.

95
00:06:31,040 --> 00:06:35,727
The diagnostic is two timings. First, time fifty batches through the loader alone,

96
00:06:35,747 --> 00:06:37,900
with no GPU work at all.

97
00:06:37,970 --> 00:06:41,998
Then time fifty forward-and-backward passes on synthetic data, bypassing the loader

98
00:06:42,018 --> 00:06:47,518
entirely. If the loader time is larger than the compute time, you are data bound,

99
00:06:47,538 --> 00:06:50,830
and no model optimization will help until that's fixed.

100
00:06:50,900 --> 00:06:55,628
The workshop's quiz: loader forty-five milliseconds, compute nine. The GPU works for

101
00:06:55,648 --> 00:06:59,980
nine and waits for thirty-six. Eighty percent of its time, wasted.

102
00:07:00,050 --> 00:07:05,326
The fix usually lives in four DataLoader settings. Workers: CPU processes loading in

103
00:07:05,346 --> 00:07:09,807
parallel. Pin memory: page-locked RAM for fast transfer. Persistent workers: don't

104
00:07:09,827 --> 00:07:15,103
respawn them every epoch. And prefetch factor: how many batches each worker loads

105
00:07:15,123 --> 00:07:15,510
ahead.

106
00:07:15,580 --> 00:07:19,666
Typical starting values. Workers: CPUs per task minus one, never zero. Pin memory:

107
00:07:19,686 --> 00:07:24,087
always true on a GPU node. Persistent workers: true whenever workers are above zero.

108
00:07:24,107 --> 00:07:28,824
Prefetch: start at two. The single biggest win in the whole workshop is one line:

109
00:07:28,844 --> 00:07:33,562
num workers from zero, the PyTorch default, to four or more. That alone often takes

110
00:07:33,582 --> 00:07:36,720
a job from twenty percent utilization to sixty or eighty.

111
00:07:36,790 --> 00:07:40,321
Two percentages on the same screen that mean different things. Utilization: the

112
00:07:40,341 --> 00:07:44,760
share of the last second the GPU was running. Low means idle, from data or

113
00:07:44,780 --> 00:07:48,312
synchronization. A hundred percent sustained is fine. Memory: the share of VRAM

114
00:07:48,332 --> 00:07:53,047
allocated. Low means you could hold a bigger batch. High is only bad near the edge,

115
00:07:53,067 --> 00:07:55,710
where the job runs out of memory and crashes.

116
00:07:55,780 --> 00:08:01,203
So read them as a pair. Low and low: the GPU is doing nothing; verify CUDA, check

117
00:08:01,223 --> 00:08:05,365
the data. Low utilization, high memory: loaded but not running; fix the pipeline.

118
00:08:05,385 --> 00:08:09,208
High utilization, low memory: healthy, with VRAM unused; increase the batch size.

119
00:08:09,227 --> 00:08:13,370
High and high: you're in good shape. And an out-of-memory crash: mixed precision

120
00:08:13,390 --> 00:08:13,690
first.

121
00:08:13,760 --> 00:08:17,744
Batch size moves both axes. Doubling it roughly doubles throughput, as long as the

122
00:08:17,764 --> 00:08:22,034
GPU isn't already saturated and the memory is there. On a B200 with one hundred

123
00:08:22,054 --> 00:08:25,466
ninety-two gigabytes, memory sitting at fifteen or twenty percent means room for

124
00:08:25,486 --> 00:08:30,328
four to eight times the batch. Find the ceiling by binary search: try a size, see if

125
00:08:30,348 --> 00:08:34,046
it fits, halve the gap, repeat. Then run production at seventy to eighty-five

126
00:08:34,066 --> 00:08:35,190
percent of that ceiling.

127
00:08:35,260 --> 00:08:39,427
Mixed precision is the next lever. PyTorch defaults to thirty-two-bit floats. Both

128
00:08:39,447 --> 00:08:44,312
AICR GPU families have tensor cores built for lower precision. The change is one

129
00:08:44,332 --> 00:08:49,197
wrapper: autocast to b float sixteen around the forward pass and the loss. Gradients

130
00:08:49,217 --> 00:08:51,640
stay in thirty-two bit on their own.

131
00:08:51,710 --> 00:08:56,655
Why BF16 and not FP16. A sixteen-bit number splits its bits between exponent, which

132
00:08:56,675 --> 00:09:01,266
is range, and mantissa, which is precision. FP32 has eight exponent bits and

133
00:09:01,286 --> 00:09:05,876
twenty-three of mantissa. BF16 keeps the same eight exponent bits and keeps only

134
00:09:05,896 --> 00:09:10,841
seven for precision, so gradients don't underflow and you need no loss scaler. FP16

135
00:09:10,861 --> 00:09:15,806
bets the other way: ten bits of precision, but only five of exponent, which

136
00:09:15,826 --> 00:09:20,417
underflows. FP8 goes further still: four and three. Every step down trades numerical

137
00:09:20,437 --> 00:09:22,190
quality for speed and memory.

138
00:09:22,260 --> 00:09:26,728
What BF16 buys on this hardware. Activation memory roughly halves, so a much bigger

139
00:09:26,748 --> 00:09:31,537
batch fits. Matrix operations run two to four times faster on the tensor cores. And

140
00:09:31,557 --> 00:09:36,345
it is mixed precision, which is the safety net: the risky parts stay in thirty-two

141
00:09:36,365 --> 00:09:39,871
bit. Master weights, the loss, softmax, normalization. Autocast handles that for

142
00:09:39,891 --> 00:09:44,359
you. So the memory saving is partial, and the speedup is largest for matrix

143
00:09:44,379 --> 00:09:44,680
multiplies.

144
00:09:44,750 --> 00:09:48,525
If memory is still too tight, gradient checkpointing trades compute for memory.

145
00:09:48,545 --> 00:09:51,688
Activations aren't stored in the forward pass; they're recomputed during

146
00:09:51,708 --> 00:09:55,167
backpropagation. Roughly sixty to seventy percent less activation memory for about

147
00:09:55,187 --> 00:09:59,595
thirty percent more compute. In Hugging Face it's one call. In custom modules, wrap

148
00:09:59,615 --> 00:10:04,340
the expensive layers. Use it when the batch is forced below eight, or when you're

149
00:10:04,360 --> 00:10:06,870
fine-tuning a very large model on one card.

150
00:10:06,940 --> 00:10:12,048
Multi-GPU is the first thing people reach for when a job is slow, and it's often the

151
00:10:12,068 --> 00:10:16,271
wrong move. More GPUs amplify the efficiency you already have; they don't create it.

152
00:10:16,291 --> 00:10:20,494
The eighty percent rule: if a single GPU is already above eighty percent, you're

153
00:10:20,514 --> 00:10:24,717
compute bound, and adding cards divides the work. If it's below sixty, fix the

154
00:10:24,737 --> 00:10:28,639
bottleneck first, because four starved GPUs waste four times as much. And an

155
00:10:28,659 --> 00:10:32,560
I/O-bound job gets worse, since the cards now compete for the same files.

156
00:10:32,630 --> 00:10:37,608
Here's what data-parallel training does. Each GPU holds a full copy of the model and

157
00:10:37,628 --> 00:10:41,607
takes a different mini-batch. After the backward pass they all-reduce: they average

158
00:10:41,627 --> 00:10:46,272
their gradients into one, and each applies the same update. Inside one node, over

159
00:10:46,292 --> 00:10:50,604
NVLink, that exchange is fast. Across nodes it's slower, so single-node multi-GPU is

160
00:10:50,624 --> 00:10:53,270
usually much faster per card than spreading out.

161
00:10:53,340 --> 00:10:58,833
Before you ask for four GPUs, confirm your code is written for DDP. A plain script

162
00:10:58,853 --> 00:11:04,001
with gres equals gpu four gets four cards and uses one. Check that distributed is

163
00:11:04,021 --> 00:11:08,480
available and initialized, and that PyTorch sees as many devices as you requested.

164
00:11:08,550 --> 00:11:14,222
Then the request: one task per GPU, four GPUs, eight CPUs for each GPU's workers,

165
00:11:14,242 --> 00:11:16,120
and run it with torchrun.

166
00:11:16,190 --> 00:11:20,928
And measure the scaling. Throughput divided by the number of GPUs times the

167
00:11:20,948 --> 00:11:24,954
single-GPU baseline gives efficiency. Above eighty percent, the extra cards pay.

168
00:11:24,974 --> 00:11:29,712
Seventy to eighty is acceptable overhead. Below seventy, communication or I/O is the

169
00:11:29,732 --> 00:11:34,470
real bottleneck, and more GPUs won't help. Go back to the single card.

170
00:11:34,540 --> 00:11:39,059
Every section was one bottleneck. In practice they come in that order of frequency,

171
00:11:39,079 --> 00:11:42,625
so here's the loop. Baseline: fifty steps, record throughput and utilization.

172
00:11:42,645 --> 00:11:46,192
Profile twenty steps. Diagnose: data, memory, compute, or communication. Fix one

173
00:11:46,212 --> 00:11:50,731
thing. Only one, or you can't tell what helped. Re-profile: did the bottleneck move?

174
00:11:50,751 --> 00:11:55,270
Record the gain. Repeat until utilization is above eighty, or the returns run out.

175
00:11:55,340 --> 00:11:59,931
The decision guide, as a tree. Utilization under fifty? It's the data pipeline, and

176
00:11:59,951 --> 00:12:04,541
if workers is zero, that first. Fifty to eighty? Look at memory. Under forty

177
00:12:04,561 --> 00:12:08,823
percent: bigger batch. Forty to eighty-five: try BF16. Over ninety: smaller batch or

178
00:12:08,843 --> 00:12:12,445
checkpointing. Memory fine? Profile. Above eighty with healthy memory? Consider more

179
00:12:12,465 --> 00:12:17,385
GPUs only if the single card is truly saturated. And out of memory: BF16, then

180
00:12:17,405 --> 00:12:20,020
checkpointing, then a smaller batch. In that order.

181
00:12:20,090 --> 00:12:24,577
After a batch job finishes, read its report card. Jobstats, from Princeton Research

182
00:12:24,597 --> 00:12:29,084
Computing, runs on AICR. Here is a real one, from a fifty-five-minute fine-tuning

183
00:12:29,104 --> 00:12:33,591
job on an RTX card. GPU utilization eighty percent: healthy. GPU memory seventeen

184
00:12:33,611 --> 00:12:37,752
percent: room for a bigger batch. CPU utilization thirteen percent: eight cores

185
00:12:37,772 --> 00:12:42,952
requested, barely one used. And a note: the job needed eleven percent of the eight

186
00:12:42,972 --> 00:12:48,153
hours it asked for. Request less time, and the queue moves faster for everyone. Make

187
00:12:48,173 --> 00:12:52,660
this a habit: under sixty percent GPU, fix it before the next run.

188
00:12:52,730 --> 00:12:56,537
The pre-submission checklist, ideally run in a devel session first. The verify

189
00:12:56,557 --> 00:13:01,001
script passes. NVIDIA S M I shows over seventy percent during a fifty-step test.

190
00:13:01,021 --> 00:13:06,103
Workers is not zero. Pin memory is on. The batch uses at least sixty percent of

191
00:13:06,123 --> 00:13:11,206
VRAM. BF16 autocast is on. The script names the right partition. CPUs per task is at

192
00:13:11,226 --> 00:13:15,670
least four and matches workers plus one. The logs folder exists. And you've profiled

193
00:13:15,690 --> 00:13:20,134
twenty steps and read the CUDA column. Then cancel the devel session: four running

194
00:13:20,154 --> 00:13:24,280
jobs per user is the limit, and an idle one blocks you too.

195
00:13:24,350 --> 00:13:29,000
None of this is PyTorch-only. Deep learning: every section applies, and in Hugging

196
00:13:29,020 --> 00:13:34,388
Face most fixes are one flag. Molecular dynamics: verify the GPU type before a long

197
00:13:34,408 --> 00:13:39,417
run, and multi-GPU scales well within a node. LLM inference: RTX cards for smaller

198
00:13:39,437 --> 00:13:43,369
models, B200s for the seventy-billion class. Custom CUDA or Triton kernels:

199
00:13:43,389 --> 00:13:48,039
everything applies, plus N-sight Compute for the kernel itself. And help is one

200
00:13:48,059 --> 00:13:51,990
email away: r c help at northeastern dot e d u.

201
00:13:52,060 --> 00:13:56,561
For Hugging Face Transformers the whole workshop is five lines of training

202
00:13:56,581 --> 00:14:01,083
arguments: BF16 on, gradient checkpointing if you need it, eight data loader

203
00:14:01,103 --> 00:14:02,590
workers, and pinned memory.

204
00:14:02,660 --> 00:14:06,647
Let's recap with Claude. Verify the GPU before anything else, because idle

205
00:14:06,667 --> 00:14:10,987
allocations on AICR get cancelled. Watch NVIDIA S M I: under fifty percent

206
00:14:11,007 --> 00:14:15,662
utilization means something is wrong. Profile twenty steps, and the top row by CUDA

207
00:14:15,682 --> 00:14:20,670
time is where to look. Low utilization with high memory is data starvation: fix the

208
00:14:20,690 --> 00:14:25,344
number of workers. High utilization with low memory: raise the batch size. Out of

